VGATE9 does not talk to a remote hypervisor. It must run on the Proxmox host itself, as root, and it calls that host’s local VM CLI (/usr/sbin/qm by default). There is no API URL or node name in .env to point at another box.

.env only changes local knobs: bind, port, PAM service, mock, read-only, CLI path, session length.

Move it to another host

1. Copy the project (or git clone) onto the other Proxmox node.
2. On that node:

cd /path/to/mithubvirt
python3 -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .
cp .env.example .env   # optional
python -m mithubvirt

3. Sign in with a local PAM user on that host (that machine’s root / gs / etc.), not the account from this box.
4. Open http://<new-host-ip>:8787.

If you use systemd

systemd/vgate9.service currently hardcodes this machine:

• WorkingDirectory=/home/gs/Projects/mithubvirt
• ExecStart=/home/gs/Projects/mithubvirt/.venv/bin/python -m mithubvirt

On the new host, change those two paths (and EnvironmentFile if you use .env), then:

cp systemd/vgate9.service /etc/systemd/system/vgate9.service
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now vgate9

What you do not put in .env

• Other node IP / hostname
• Proxmox API token
• Cluster name

Those are unused. Each install only manages VMs on the host where the process is running.
